d on which mountain so that  is how I drifted from science into  mainstream journalism the Panos  Institute was based at that time in  London and they had offices in Africa  Latin America and and also Asia I helped  set up the South Asian Bureau and office  of Panos Pano's basically dealt with  training people in communications on  specific fields like thematic areas like  trans boundary water or environment or  gender or hiv/aids or TB stories with  the soft focus which the mainstream  media was not really that interested in  so how do you bring the expertise among  journalists in this thematic areas up to  speed so that what they write is  credible and and written with with  expertise so we worked on that for about  two years and panelist was a it's a very  good place to work with a whole lot of  journalists in India and Nepal Pakistan  Bangladesh Sri Lanka where the problems  are very similar and where journalists  could actually learn from each other a  lot and of course water being one of the  thematic areas we worked on was also  linked rivers don't respect man-made  boundaries they flow across borders and  cross countries  and and we needed to look at watersheds  and an entire environment from a more  trans boundary perspective and and on  with panels we did we had fellowships so  that Bangladesh India and Nepal  journalists could work together for  example on the Ganges basin and we took  them on field trips down from the  Chinese border through Nepal through  India right down to the Ganges and  Brahmaputra Delta so then they got a  perspective that all water is different  you know water you really have to look  at it as a regional natural resource I  think unfortunately the the media in  Nepal and around the world actually  don't really understand the the science  of energy and also the politics of  energy it's reported as just as another  beat and usually it's the economic or  business beat but energy has very very  close relationship with the environment 