olitically volatile that it affects  their internal politics and and India  for example would need stability in  order to cooperate with Nepal on sharing  of the river waters and and building  joint projects so India's main concern  in Nepal is water and and it's linked  directly to bilateral relation relations  between Kathmandu and Delhi with China  they need stability so that a volatile  Nepal will not be a springboard for  Tibetan nationalism and and China's  China looks at Nepal almost solely  through Tibetan prism and since Tibet is  such an important sensitive issue for  China they don't want the power to be a  problem  and as long as India understands and  accepts the annexation of Tibet which it  does China doesn't see India as a  problem visa vie Tibet I think to look  at the conflict in Nepal 1996 to 2006  you really have to go back to well you  have to go back right to our feudal  history but I think up to 1990 when we  had our first People Power  demonstrations on the street the  pro-democracy movement forced the king  at that time to allow political parties  to function and we had our first  multi-party elections in 1991 those  tremendous euphoria of democracy because  remember Nepal had been a absolute  monarchy a dictatorship for 30 years  from 1960 to 1990 and so from 1990 we  felt that wow we have democracy now  democracynow.org  very quickly and by 1995 there was a lot  of disillusionment among Nepal ease that  democracy was not really providing  prosperity and equity and all the other  things that we expected it would and and  one group of communists who are inspired  by Mao Zedong in neighboring China felt  that shortcut to to power would be  through a violent struggle against the  state and at that time it was against  the monarchy a constitutional monarchy  so they started their the revolution in  the mountains of Midwestern Nepal  following a lot of mouths teachings of  the strategy of revolution learning a  lot from Peru and the Sendero Luminoso  and how they conducted their revolution  and the the the objective conditions in  Nepal with feudalism  equity's lack of social justice was so  pervasive that the the revolution spread  like a wildfire I think beyond the  imagination of Maori that the Maoist did  not realize it was spread so quickly so  it was very easy for them to recruit  because if you talked about  discrimination and and injustice people  who you know wholeheartedly just just  joined the movement and of course the  the state's initial crackdown a very  brutal crackdown on the on the  revolutionaries didn't help because it  pushed ordinary people into the Maoist  fold so that's how it all started but I  think the Maoist would perhaps impatient  yes there was injustice yes there was a  lot of exclusion of marginalized  communities but democracy and especially  grassroots democracy in Nepal with the  decentralization and the self-governance  acts was starting to yield results for  the first time in Nepal local elections  for village councils and district  councils was directly leading to  accountable local leaders who were  forced to perform which means to provide  services like health education jobs  roads to the people if they wanted to be  elected again so it was starting to work  and then the war came at the same time  and I don't think it's a coincidence  that the first target of the Maoist  revolutionaries were precisely these  elected local leaders because they were  competing for the same constituency so  at by the end of the conflict in 2006  nearly 85% of all the village council  buildings had been destroyed and bombed  village council members were hounded out  of the of the villages many of them are  living in the district capitals or in  Kathmandu so something that was started  with a sort of a utopian dream of a  better Nepal basically ended up  brutalizing society setting back  development and I'm not saying that  there was no achieve  and I think it did it did make a lot of  people aware of their rights especially  the women marginalized groups but it  also let them down and as we have seen  in in the elections this week the the  disillusionment against the Maoist party  even within Maori scada has been very  very high so by 1995 media activists had  lobbied successfully with the government  to allow the radio spectrum which they  regarded as a commons to be taken away  from the state control and I think that  was a landmark thing radio sagarmatha  which was Nepal's first community radio  station was started in 1996 and that  opened the door for community radios to  come out and I think as far as community  radio is concerned an apology is right  ahead of all the countries in the region  I had even of India India with its you  know history of democracy and Free Press  actually controls radio with an iron  hand you are not allowed to broadcast  news even if you're a private radio  station all news is centralized from the  state radio whereas in a ballots there  are nearly 400 FM stations they're owned  by local village councils by NGOs by  community groups there are also  commercial stations but everyone is  forced to be act like a community radio  so I think this and this has  complemented democracy at the grassroots  I think the communication element to  democracy is given to Nepal and the  police through community radio because  they can't be democracy without  participation and for participation you  need communication and especially  communication at the grassroots meaning  decentralized media and I think this is  where Nepal is ahead of other countries  well in the beginning the Nepali media  treated the conflict and the start of  the revolution in these remote mountains  of Midwestern Nepal as if it was  happening on some other planet it was  treated like a ordinary law-and-order  story policemen killed here some attacks  there  it wasn't linked to a larger revolution  that was starting and igniting in the  mountains and Kathmandu couldn't have  been further away  reporters were lazy they didn't really  go out they didn't have the resources or  the energy or maybe the the interest to  go and report on what was happening  there and perhaps because of that when  the revolution spread rapidly and  district Capitol started being attacked  in let's say 2000 people in Kathmandu  were shocked the politicians Wow  this is serious business so I think the  media was part of the problem I think in  in making people aware that there was a  serious violent conflict starting in the  country and by the time policymakers  media and social society in Kathmandu  realize what was going on it was already  quite a serious war and I think as far  as for me personally the conflict and  reporting on the conflict I had been  writing and reporting on conflicts in  other parts of Asia I reported on the  Sri Lanka war at a time when there were  two civil wars going on at the same time  the Tamil separatist war in the north  and the Marxist revolution in the south  with the jvp I did Philippines the  aftermath of the Cambodian genocide and  so forth and coming back to Nepal I  really felt that you had to do  journalism differently if you if media  was going to be a part of the solution  in finding resolution to the conflict as  well as helping in the reconciliation  process  but somehow the rules of journalism  don't allow you to do that we are taught  at journalism schools to be spectators  not to be involved not to be outraged by  human rights violations for example and  you have to take that objective view of  what's happening little aloof that  doesn't work  I think if media is going to be a part  of the solution you really have to be  involved and I felt the limiting that  that the rules of journalism actually  limit you in getting closer to the truth  of what the conflict was doing  especially to civilians  so we tried in our newspaper and  magazine too to cover human rights  violations to do two more field  reporting to go out to the countryside  and find out how the conflict was  affecting civilians but even then there  was a feeling of inadequacy which is why  we started work on those on the on the  books on the conflict the war was coming  to its tenth anniversary it looked like  it was never going to end  and we really thought we could tell the  story of what the conflict had done to  us to Nepal ease and the country in  general through pictures through  photography I'm not a photographer  myself but I felt the power of  photography was very strong to tell the  story so we started putting together and  collecting photographs from Nepali  journalists foreign photo journalists  who had been to Nepal during the  conflict  even ordinary villagers were taking  pictures with with the very simple  cameras so and by the time the book came  out the war had ended and a lot of  people told us oh now now there's no  need for a photo book on the conflict  because you know we have peace but as  you know the end of the war did not mean  peace and the legacy of violence  continued and so the book was very  important not just as an exercise in  memory of the violence but also to try  to prevent such violence  again in future but the story told  through the medium of photography and so  the first book Larry magenta which means  people wore came out in in January 2007  it was an immediate bestseller and it  was just supposed to be one book but the  publisher decided not to make any money  from it  Nepal ayah and we said okay from the  proceeds of the sale of the book why  don't we take the pictures to the people  so we went on a traveling photo  exhibition around Nepal showed it in 45  of Nepal 75 districts by the end of it  half a million people had seen the  pictures and it's amazing it's not as  the quantity of people the number of  people who came to see but the intensity  of feeling with which they looked at the  photographs and saw themselves in it  their own war experiences and then 