r could conceivably  have killed each other during battles so  this was very very powerful stuff and  you couldn't have made feature films or  documentaries or write novels about many  of these characters in the first book so  I think this whole project which grew  organically it wasn't planned like this  it was just supposed to be one book in  the beginning grew from a photo archive  of the conflict into almost a peace  movement and I think we were nudged  along pushed along by the people and the  desire for peace and justice and the  need to remember not to forget what the  war did to us you know I made a living  being a skeptic of new information  technologies the internet social  networking I said how is this going to  be a panacea how is this going to level  the playing field if our schools don't  have roof  on them how is sticking a computer into  a school which doesn't have teachers  which doesn't have electricity which  doesn't have textbooks going to help so  we are we are getting you know ahead of  ourselves that was my feeling and and I  think the digital divide within Nepal is  even more acute than it is globally  eighty percent of the internet sites  registered in Nepal are within the Ring  Road in Kathmandu so I was a skeptic and  then I think one day I had this moment  of Epiphany where through my smartphone  I could transmit a picture I had taken  off of a lake and mountains in a remote  part of northwestern about to the world  right from there two weeks walk from the  nearest road and yet telecommunications  had bridged that gap so the digital  divide obviously was getting was  shrinking and there was tremendous  potential for education and media and  communications with this technology and  so now I'm sort of somewhat of a  born-again and digerati you can see the  potential and and for media definitely  there is great value in magnifying your  audience as a reporter as an editor I  find directing people to come and read  what I write now it's possible through  social networking and to let people know  and discuss its dynamic content its  interactive it can be multimedia so just  this extends the the role of media a  mainstream media but of course  everything is changing it's not  journalists who are journalists now  there's also citizen journalism anyone  with a smartphone is now a journalist if  you're at the right place at the right  time  you can upload right from wherever you  are even in a country like Nepal and  things are changing exponentially we  don't realize how fast it is changing  one year ago I used to tell people at  seminars there are two million people in  Nepal with Facebook today I have to say  it  3.2 million 1.2 million people have been  added in one year with Facebook accounts  even in a country like Nepal it's going  up like that the internet penetration  rate is rising the people with mobile  phones is now sixty percent and as more  and more people get smartphones I think  the internet penetration rate is also  going to go up more people are going to  be accessing the net through their  smartphones than through their pcs or  laptops and the revolution that has  happened let's say in America Europe in  India even two or three years ago is  it's very rapidly coming to Nepal and we  really need so for media and journalists  to be trained in the technology they're  training at the moment is not adequate  people don't understand the role  Facebook is used mainly to be in touch  with friends the journalists are getting  into Twitter but there is media  education is lagging behind and media  education not just for journalists we  tend to think all media education is for  reporters to learn about social network  no it's for the public the public needs  to know how the media operates who owns  media how it is controlled for example  and and also the sensitivities of  smartphones and and new social  networking a lot of very a lot of young  men and women in Nepal as elsewhere in  the world have g