noso  and how they conducted their revolution  and the the the objective conditions in  Nepal with feudalism  equity's lack of social justice was so  pervasive that the the revolution spread  like a wildfire I think beyond the  imagination of Maori that the Maoist did  not realize it was spread so quickly so  it was very easy for them to recruit  because if you talked about  discrimination and and injustice people  who you know wholeheartedly just just  joined the movement and of course the  the state's initial crackdown a very  brutal crackdown on the on the  revolutionaries didn't help because it  pushed ordinary people into the Maoist  fold so that's how it all started but I  think the Maoist would perhaps impatient  yes there was injustice yes there was a  lot of exclusion of marginalized  communities but democracy and especially  grassroots democracy in Nepal with the  decentralization and the self-governance  acts was starting to yield results for  the first time in Nepal local elections  for village councils and district  councils was directly leading to  accountable local leaders who were  forced to perform which means to provide  services like health education jobs  roads to the people if they wanted to be  elected again so it was starting to work  and then the war came at the same time  and I don't think it's a coincidence  that the first target of the Maoist  revolutionaries were precisely these  elected local leaders because they were  competing for the same constituency so  at by the end of the conflict in 2006  nearly 85% of all the village council  buildings had been destroyed and bombed  village council members were hounded out  of the of the villages many of them are  living in the district capitals or in  Kathmandu so something that was started  with a sort of a utopian dream of a  better Nepal basically ended up  brutalizing society setting back  development and I'm not saying that  there was no achieve  and I think it did it did make a lot of  people aware of their rights especially  the women marginalized groups but it  also let them down and as we have seen  in in the elections this week the the  disillusionment against the Maoist party  even within Maori scada has been very  very high so by 1995 media activists had  lobbied successfully with the government  to allow the radio spectrum which they  regarded as a commons to be taken away  from the state control and I think that  was a landmark thing radio sagarmatha  which was Nepal's first community radio  station was started in 1996 and that  opened the door for community radios to  come out and I think as far as community  radio is concerned an apology is right  ahead of all the countries in the region  I had even of India India with its you  know history of democracy and Free Press  actually controls radio with an iron  hand you are not allowed to broadcast  news even if you're a private radio  station all news is centralized from the  state radio whereas in a ballots there  are nearly 400 FM stations they're owned  by local village councils by NGOs by  community groups there are also  commercial stations but everyone is  forced to be act like a community radio  so I think this and this has  complemented democracy at the grassroots  I think the communication element to  democracy is given to Nepal and the  police through community radio because  they can't be democracy without  participation and for participation you  need communication and especially  communication at the grassroots meaning  decentralized media and I think this is  where Nepal is ahead of other countries  well in the beginning the Nepali media  treated the conflict and the start of  the revolution in these remote mountains  of Midwestern Nepal as if it was  happening on some other planet it was  treated like a ordinary law-and-order  story policemen killed here some attacks  there  it wasn't linked to a larger revolution  that was starting and igniting in the  mountains and Kathmandu couldn't have  been further away  reporters were lazy they didn't really  go out they didn't have the resources or  the energy or maybe the the interest to  go and report on what was happening  there and perhaps because of that when  the revolution spread rapidly and  district Capitol started being attacked  in let's say 2000 people in Kathmandu  were shocked the politicians Wow  this is serious business so I think the  media was part of the problem I think in  in making people aware that there was a  serious violent conflict starting in the  country and by the time policymakers  media and social society in Kathmandu  realize what was going on it was already  quite a serious war and I think as far  as for me personally the conflict and  reporting on the conflict I had been  writing and reporting on conflicts in  other parts of Asia I reported on the  Sri Lanka war at a time when there were  two civil wars going on at the same time  the Tamil separatist war in the north  and the Marxist revolution in the south  with the jvp I did Philippines the  aftermath of the Cambodian genocide and  so forth and coming back to Nepal I  really felt that you had to do  journalism differently if you if media  was going to be a part of the solution  in finding resolution to the conflict as  well as helping in the reconciliation  process  but somehow the rules of journalism  don't allow you to do that we are taught  at journalism schools to be spectators  not to be involved not to be outraged by  human rights violations for example and  you have to take that objective view of  what's happening little aloof that  doesn't work  I think if media is going to be a part  of the solution you really have to be  involved and I felt the limiting that  that the rules of journalism actually  limit you in getting closer to the truth  of what the conflict was doing  especially to civilians  so we tried in our newspaper and  magazine too to cover human rights  violations to do two more field  reporting to go out to the countryside  and find out how the conflict was  affecting civilians but even then there  was a feeling of inadequacy which is why  we started work on those on the on the  books on the conflict the war was coming  to its tenth anniversary it looked like  it was never going to end  and we really thought we could tell the  story of what the conflict had done to  us to Nepal ease and the country in  general through pictures through  photography I'm not a photographer  myself but I felt the power of  photography was very strong to tell the  story so we started putting together and  collecting photographs from Nepali  journalists foreign photo journalists  who had been to Nepal during the  conflict  even ordinary villagers were taking  pictures with with the very simple  cameras so and by the time the book came  out the war had ended and a lot of  people told us oh now now there's no  need for a photo book on the conflict  because you know we have peace but as  you know the end of the war did not mean  peace and the legacy of violence  continued and so the book was very  important not just as an exercise in  memory of the violence but also to try  to prevent such violence  again in future but the story told  through the medium of photography and so  the first book Larry magenta which means  people wore came out in in January 2007  it was an immediate bestseller and it  was just supposed to be one book but the  publisher decided not to make any money  from it  Nepal ayah and we said okay from the  proceeds of the sale of the book why  don't we take the pictures to the people  so we went on a traveling photo  exhibition around Nepal showed it in 45  of Nepal 75 districts by the end of it  half a million people had seen the  pictures and it's amazing it's not as  the quantity of people the number of  people who came to see but the intensity  of feeling with which they looked at the  photographs and saw themselves in it  their own war experiences and then they  all wrote very moving testimonies after  seeing the pictures in the guest books  and we were just so powerfully moved by  those testimonies that we said we must  chronicle this so the second book came  out called never again piteous the nos  which which select some of these  comments and puts it together you know  in a in a photo book with pictures of  the exhibition tour and the testimonies  next to it and then our friend casts on  set and who's Nepal's foremost  documentary filmmaker decided to make a  film based on the on the tour and and we  took that documentary called frames of  war you dodged etre also around the  country showing it in cinema halls in  about 15 districts and while we were  showing the film we would notice in the  audience people who you recognized as  characters from the first book so and we  talked to them and we'd hear dramatic  stories of what they had been through so  the instant at which that picture was  taken in the first book was actually a  very small part of what they had  actually experienced and we said we must  tell this story  so the third book came out which is  called  you'd the Pacis anta yeah people after  war which is a before-and-after kind of  story on on the characters from for the  first book including very dramatic story  which is the cover of third book which  is of a woman gorilla who was pictured  in the first book by sagar Shrestha is  this dramatic portrait of a woman  gorilla with a gun sitting in sentry  duty in Bhojpuri in eastern nepal  brilliantly composed photographed  technically perfect and we often  wondered what has become of our way she  now and we tracked her down to a  cantonment in in eastern nepal and we  found out that her name was actually jun  arai we didn't have her name in the in  the first book and her brother  Ruben Rai was in the army so brother and  sister were on opposing sides in the  conflict so Nepal's conflict was not  just a civil war but a sibling war and  the brother and sister could conceivably  have killed each other during battles so  this was very very powerful stuff and  you couldn't have made feature films or  documentaries or write novels about many  of these characters in the first book so  I think this whole project which grew  organically it wasn't planned like this  it was just supposed to be one book in  the beginning grew from a photo archive  of the conflict into almost a peace  movement and I think we were nudged  along pushed along by the people and the  desire for peace and justice and the  need to remember not to forget what the  war did to us you know I made a living  being a skeptic of new information  technologies the internet social  networking I said how is this going to  be a panacea how is this going to level  the playing field if our schools don't  have roof  on them how is sticking a computer into  a school which doesn't have teachers  which doesn't have electricity which  doesn't have textbooks going to help so  we are we are getting you know ahead of  ourselves that was my feeling and and I  think the digital divide within Nepal is  even more acute than it is globally  eighty percent of the internet sites  registered in Nepal are within the Ring  Road in Kathmandu so I was a skeptic and  then I think one day I had this moment  of Epiphany where through my smartphone  I could transmit a picture I had taken  off of a lake and mountains in a remote  part of northwestern about to the world  right from there two weeks walk from the  nearest road and yet telecommunications  had bridged that gap so the digital  divide obviously was getting was  shrinking and there was tremendous  potential for education and media and  communications with this technology and  so now I'm sort of somewhat of a  born-again and digerati you can see the  potential and and for media definitely  there is great value in magnifying your  audience as a reporter as an editor I  find directing people to come and read  what I write now it's possible through  social networking and to let people know  and discuss its dynamic content its  interactive it can be multimedia so just  this extends the the role of media a  mainstream media but of course  everything is changing it's not  journalists who are journalists now  there's also citizen journalism anyone  with a smartphone is now a journalist if  you're at the right place at the right  time  you can upload right from wherever you  are even in a country like Nepal and  things are changing exponentially we  don't realize how fast it is changing  one year ago I used to tell people at  seminars there are two million people in  Nepal with Facebook today I have to say  it  3.2 million 1.2 million people have been  added in one year with Facebook accounts  even in a country like Nepal it's going  up like that the internet penetration  rate is rising the people with mobile  phones is now sixty percent and as more  and more people get smartphones I think  the internet penetration rate is also  going to go up more people are going to  be accessing the net through their  smartphones than through their pcs or  laptops and the revolution that has  happened let's say in America Europe in  India even two or three years ago is  it's very rapidly coming to Nepal and we  really need so for media and journalists  to be trained in the technology they're  training at the moment is not adequate  people don't understand the role  Facebook is used mainly to be in touch  with friends the journalists are getting  into Twitter but there is media  education is lagging behind and media  education not just for journalists we  tend to think all media education is for  reporters to learn about social network  no it's for the public the public needs  to know how the media operates who owns  media how it is controlled for example  and and also the sensitivities of  smartphones and and new social  networking a lot of very a lot of young  men and women in Nepal as elsewhere in  the world have got into trouble because  they don't understand the medium and  issues like privacy and so forth so we  we really need to upgrade our school  curriculum both in high school as well  as college with media literacy even if  and those people are not don't want to  be journalists they need to know  I think with social media and new media  technologies at the moment we are in a  stage where the old media paper  newspapers are not yet dead and the new  media has been born but is still a  toddler so in this sort of transition  phase and the role of paper newspapers  will still be there but the eyeballs are  moving the readers have already moved  unfortunately the advertisers haven't  moved so you still need your hardcopy  newspaper as a vehicle of revenue for  for advertising because you can't really  charge yet in Nepal for your online  content so the online is still being  subsidized by the hardcopy but I think  this will change very rapidly it has  already changed for example in India and  and if it happens in India is gonna come  very rapidly to us is that the  advertisers will also shift the only  reason advertisers haven't gone yet is  because a lot of the advertisers are  older men like me who haven't really  realized they're still watching  television at home so they think that's  the medium but as Facebook users reach 4  million 5 million I think it's going to  happen already the advertising has gone  to Facebook in Nepal and it's only a  question of time before advertising also  shifts but Nepali times actually we were  way ahead of our time because when we  started the newspaper in 2000 we started  with an online edition for the first 4  months so we were online before we were  hardcopy so now I think we'll come a  full circle at some point and become  more of an online outlet than a hardcopy  outlet already there are three times  more readers of our online site then  there is all our hard copy subscribers
 

